[Music]  hi everyone I'm Jessica cousins and I am  an AI policy specialist with the future  of life Institute as well as a research  fellow with the UC Berkeley Center for  long-term cyber security so you've  already heard really fantastic talks  from both Allen and Brian landscaping  the issues and the actors related to the  strategy and coordination and governance  of AI and AGI development so I won't  talk more about that range of issues  here just flag really quickly that the  future of life Institute does have a  resource page on AI policy which is  future of life org slash AI policy and  there's also going to be a breakout  session tomorrow I believe at 1:00 p.m.  talking about global governance of AGI  if that interests you further so what  I'm going to share now is a more brief  summary of the strategy discussion that  has taken place over the last couple  days here at the workshop the goal of  this is just transparency and help make  sure that everyone's on the same page  for the continuing discussions we'll  have over the next three days of the  beneficial AGI conference so this  morning you heard about some of the  speculative future destinations that  have gained traction from participants  here and these provide useful  inspiration to the work that we're  engaged with but given the complexity  and the diversity of what is at stake  even more important than defining the  contours of a space where we want to be  is to identify those shared goals and  commonalities among a range of diverse  actors so you've heard your you've heard  some of those commonalities across the  destinations already features such as  humility robustness flexibility autonomy  peacefulness and the reduction of  suffering and these desired features can  really help guide us towards the AGI  futures that we want and our strategy is  to get there so to make it through this  period safely and into a post AGI world  we need to keep these features in mind  but we also need to chart paths required  to help us get to futures we all enjoy  so yesterday morning we worked through a  of scenarios to help us imagine the  possibility space for example we were  asked to imagine the character of Bob  and this was from Joe Harrah's great  talks to please find him to give him the  credit he deserves for this so bob is a  leader at a major company seeking to  develop AGI he goes to meetings like  this one he knows there are strategic  challenges associated with AGI and he's  seeking advice what do you tell him  maybe the current narratives of tech  utopia on one hand and existential  threats on the other are not the most  compelling narratives so what do we have  at our disposal it was suggested that we  can look at three interconnected  elements development deployment and  landscape when it comes to development  of AGI what are the levers that matter  most for starters data so we need  responsible data use policies what kind  of information will Bob have access to  what will he share he will also need  sufficiently secure and efficient  hardware other factors that influence  development include questions related to  control incentives and openness maybe  Bob needs a charter to indicate his  willingness to be transparent about the  status of his company's technology maybe  he needs to know how to design safety  reliability and accountability into his  processes from the outset and how will  he measure resiliency across all of  these fronts  how will he protect his networks from  cyber attacks when it comes time for  deployment  Bob's concerns will only grow not only  does he need to think about all of the  issues already mentioned but he has to  assess the general generality and  capability of the systems and the  domains of their application what uses  are illegal what uses are dangerous what  uses could be co-opted or results in a  public backlash against his company as  for the landscape Bob probably needs to  accept that his company does not exist  in a vacuum but that he has to assess  his position along  a complex landscape of actors social  cultural and environmental realities  economic constraints and security  considerations  does he have partnerships with Amazon or  Google is his work supported by his  national government might people see his  tool as a symbol of change they don't  want leading to the equivalent of  throwing rocks at the Google buses what  if he ends up automating people's jobs  will foreign governments see his  development as a threat obviously bob  has a lot to think about  but really as Bob's advisors we have a  lot to think about how can we boil down  our recommendations and improve our  messaging so that he quickly understands  the difficulties and steps he can take  to help create more beneficial AGI in  the workshop we focused on scenario  roleplay as a mechanism through which to  explore the motivations of different  actors and the kinds of developments  that increase the chances of global  coordination as was pointed out being  Bob for an hour can make you a better  Bob advisor so we split into many  different groups and played out numerous  scenarios intended to depict a variety  of possible probable and surprising  futures in which each person took on the  role of different actors influencing the  scenario the insights from these  breakouts are really interesting people  found that the fall to negative outcomes  happens quickly and could be the  decision of just one prominent actor  they also had frustrations that they  didn't have more tools to leverage even  if they held a position of seemingly  great power people struggle to find ways  to credibly commit to global or  bilateral coordination with existing  paradigms and in some scenarios the  gradual and subtle use of AGI prevented  other actors from knowing of its  existence and being able to make  proactive decisions about how to respond  in scenarios where one country's  industry and government were not working  together efficiently they experienced a  harder time responding to global threats  P  also found that if top AI researchers  could communicate one coherent message  about risks to their government it  worked a lot better than having that  community display on the more positive  side people found that implementing  concrete channels for monitoring and  communication increased overall feelings  of agency moreover having shared  knowledge of mutually harmful effects  contributed to coordination as did the  perception that everyone was relatively  aware of the capabilities of their  adversaries one group found that the  desire to build technology faster  actually led to greater global  coordination because researchers needed  to share and discuss their findings they  instituted an AI Olympics to further  propel innovation overall many groups  found the majority of global players  were incentivized to cooperate in order  to realize the benefits of the  technologies and prevent an increasing  number of catastrophes so stepping back  into reality there was also a really  great panel yesterday that elucidated  key issues to be aware of in the  strategy space participants highlighted  the need to think about who the relevant  actors are and how stable that  configuration is into the future to  question the institutions and the  processes determining the rules and  whether it's actually working well for  people and how to determine how broadly  to share results in sensitive research  participants also talked about how to  facilitate global coordination and  suggested that current institutions are  not sufficient and many people feel  disconnected and even alienated from  their political systems it was also  pointed out that emerging laws for AI  like the bot disclosure act in  California for example might be  important practice for the community -  in supporting the application of rules  to ml systems so participants of the  workshop have proposed a variety of  action items and these should be carried  into the conference for consideration as  we all continue to identify further  action items over the next three days so  there have been tons of really great  ideas proposed and I'm not gonna go into  all of them now just give just share a  few ideas that have come out  that were mentioned fairly consistently  or that got some positive feedback so  one participant emphasized the  importance of targeted conferences so  you can all feel good about being here  and other ideas have included blockchain  coordination tools anonymous markets for  whistleblowing establishing new AI  narratives in the media credibly  signaling trust from AI organizations  establishing a unified framework of AI  social responsibility that is trackable  facilitating the use of ml and Social  Sciences and civic tech instituting a  new governance regime that implements  private regulators who are designated by  the government identifying the early  wins in AI policy that translate to more  advanced systems for example by  implementing standards for transparency  and accountability improving  documentation of impact and performance  evaluation of AML models leveraging the  many areas of commonality across AI  policy around the world to support  international cooperation and we've  heard some great areas of commonality  between the US and China and Brian's  talk establishing rules for the use of  AI and militaries and critical  decision-making and enabling commitments  for coordination and resource sharing  prior to the emergence of AGI and the  windfall Clause that Ellen mentioned is  another great example of that so if any  of these ideas spark interest for you  feel free to find me I can connect you  to the right person and then enter your  own ideas on the Google spreadsheet so  across all of the ideas that people have  proposed so far for strategically  reaching our goals a number of themes  have already emerged the first one is  that there are actions to be taken by a  broad diversity of actors there have  been actions identified for people in AI  research and development in operations  and communications in academia and civil  society and in the government so  hopefully that fact encourages you that  whatever brought you here however you  got here there are paths being  highlighted that can help you identify  ways to support safe and beneficial AGI  a second theme that emerged was to  identify the comparative advantage that  you may have and target your actions  toward those with significant impact our  opportunity and a final theme of the  actions proposed is that many of them  require at least some degree of  institutional change whether to  establish greater accountability within  organizations or even to rethink the way  that we govern technology altogether so  I think that points to the difficulty of  the task ahead of us but I'd guess that  this group might be up to the challenge  thank you  [Applause]  [Music]

